  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe repurpose a combination of gradient-based optimization and adaptive resolution technique to carefully navigate the discrete function space. This approach aims to overcome the limitations of the provided parent solution by:\n   - Implementing a two-phase local search strategy at higher resolutions\n   - Using analytic gradient information for more efficient optimization\n   - Maintaining a stability mechanism to recover from numerical issues\n   - Introducing a sophisticated parent transfer mechanism with higher-order interpolation\n\nThe core bottleneck addressed is the inefficiency of low-resolution optimization in the complex function space, solved by:\n   a. Using gradient-based optimization (SLSQP) with exact derivatives through vectorization\n   b. Increasing resolution progressively to maintain solution quality\n   c. Implementing a stability layer to keep the solution physically meaningful\n   d. Using interpolation that preserves integral constraints during resolution transfers\n\nKey improvements from previous approaches include analytic gradient calculation and more robust constraint handling. The method intentionally maintains conciseness to stay within time and code limits.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe represent the function h as piecewise constant on [0,2] with N points. The nonlinear objective to minimize is:\n   C_5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nwith domain shift k allowed. This is discretized as:\n   ||ops||\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   A. Initialization\n   - Start with given parent solution (or random if unavailable)\n   - Set base resolution to 20 to reduce initial optimization cost\n   - Ensure integral constraint by projection operator\n\n   B. Local Optimization Subroutine\n   - Define analytic gradient of the objective \n   - Use SLSQP with bounds [0,1] and integral constraint\n   - Apply iterative projection to enforce physical constraints\n\n   C. Resolution Expansion Mechanism\n   - Systematically double resolution when improvement threshold is met\n   - Use PCHIP interpolation to maintain function smoothness\n   - Validate gradient calculations at new resolutions\n\n   D. Global Selection Policy\n   - Maintain active candidate history across resolutions\n   - Prioritize improvement over raw score reduction\n   - Limit computational time by adaptive evaluation scheduling\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Default resolution progression: 20, 40, 80, 160, 320, 400 steps\n   - Objective function parameters: scale factor 100 balancing exploration/exploitation\n   - Integral constraint projection iterations: 200 with tolerance 1e-6\n   - Time limits per phase: 30% for optimization, 70% for resolution advancement\n\n   Adaptive choices:\n     * Tighten bounds aggressively if score plateaus\n     * Reduce function evaluations if correlation with parent diminishes\n     * Maintain separate pools of perturbed solutions at each resolution\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Time estimation: T(n) \u221d n\u00b3 for gradient-based correlation\n   - Wall-clock monitoring: 5% buffer for convergence assessment\n   - Time recovery: back-step resolution if dead-end detected\n   - Integral recovery: automatic constraint restoration\n\n   Fallback strategy:\n     * Keep parent solution viable if optimization fails\n     * Revert to last verified solution\n     * Maintain last improvement record\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Check physical constraints (sum h = n/2)\n   - Validate objective against original integral definition\n   - Return interpolated representation for final solution\n   - Compute performance via:\n        score = (max(np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')) * 2.0/len(h))\n   Additional safety checks:\n     * Constraint enforcement (explicit at each developer)\n     * Derivative consistency checks\n     * Bounds verification\n\nAll components designed to be self-starting and independent of external libraries beyond scipy/numpy.",
